     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. A dose of 4250 mg three times daily (12,750 mg/day) is about 5 times the maximum daily dose. It could cause life-threatening lactic acidosis. Nothing in this patient's profile, such as normal kidney and liver function, makes that dose safe.\n\nHere is what I'd suggest:\n- **If it's a typo:** Tell me the intended dose. For example, 500 mg PO twice daily with meals is a standard start, and 850 mg PO three times daily is the usual maximum. I'll enter it right away.\n- **If you really intend this dose:** Please talk to your supervising resident, attending, or pharmacist first. If a prescriber still wants a dose above the normal range, they can enter it directly or give me a documented rationale. Pharmacy will probably still need to verify it.\n- **If a dose was already taken:** If the patient might have received or taken an excessive amount, tell your team now. Metformin overdose needs urgent evaluation, including lactate and acid-base status.\n\nI can enter a corrected order as soon as you give me one."
      }
     ],
